\subsection{Cryptocurrency Forecasting}
There is growing interest in cryptocurrencies, particularly with Bitcoin leading the way as the first digital currency \cite{nakamoto}. 
% As a new asset class, the traditional findings related a weak form efficiency in financial markets may not directly apply to it. 
% Several studies have evaluated the level of market efficiency in the Bitcoin market over different time periods. For example, Urquhart \emph{et al.} \cite{urquhart} examined daily Bitcoin prices from August 2010 to July 2016 and determined that the Bitcoin market lacks weak form efficiency. Other researchers \cite{nadarajah} have found evidence supporting the weak form efficient market hypothesis for Bitcoin returns after applying specific power transformations. Similarly, Bariviera \emph{et al.} \cite{bariviera} analyzed daily Bitcoin prices from August 2011 to February 2017 using the Hurst exponent and revealed that the Bitcoin market was not weak form efficient before 2014, but became weak form efficient after that point. In summary, there is conflicting evidence regarding the efficiency of the Bitcoin market, but most studies indicated that it has become more efficient over time, possibly due to increased competition and market growth. \\
\sloppy
Studies have assessed Bitcoin’s market efficiency over time with mixed findings. Urquhart \cite{urquhart} found inefficiency from 2010–2016, while Nadarajah \cite{nadarajah} observed weak form efficiency using power transformations. Likewise, Bariviera \cite{bariviera} showed Bitcoin market was not weak form efficient before 2014, but became weak form efficient afterwards. Overall, evidence is mixed, though many suggest Bitcoin has become more efficient as the market grows.

