% We took particular care of the evaluation methods in our work. In the literature, this part is often overlooked and the approaches do not have the solidity that makes them safe to be employed on real data. 

% For a highly non-stationary dataset, a rolling window approach for cross-validation is essential to average results across different historical scenarios. The rolling window setup keeps the training region size constant, adjusting the start as the forecast origin moves forward, dropping the oldest data for new observations. This approach is especially useful for pure auto regressive machine learning models.
% We chose a rolling window approach over expanding to ensure each model is evaluated on the same number of training points. We split the data into windows beforehand to run methods across different sections of the price history, allowing us to analyze variations in results. We evaluated windows with training sizes of 15, 30, and 60 points, keeping the test size at 10 points and the stride at 1. 
For a highly non-stationary dataset, we use a rolling-window cross-validation approach. The training set size remains fixed while the window slides forward, dropping the oldest data for each new forecast. This ensures that each model is evaluated on the same number of training points. We pre-split the data into windows with training sizes of 15, 30, and 60 points, a test size of 10 points, and a stride of 1.
% The test set remained short to avoid performance issues due to non-stationarity and to manage computational time. This resulted in three experiments with a total of 78, 48, and 27 windows, respectively.

%\textbf{Additional observations}. The above rolling window setup remains valid and applicable for XGBoost, even though it is a tabular model. \\ Note also that, both XGBoost and ESN can forecast multiple consecutive points ahead, but this would be counterproductive. In fact, the learned price dynamics would be applied to points that are ahead in the future and this would deteriorate the predictions. A solution to this would be to use the ESN in closed-loop generative mode, but we have not explored this possibility in this study. For the same reason, we decided to not set buffers between the training and test sets, which would avoid a potential information leakage, as described in \cite{10reas}. This procedure is called purging and embargoing, depending on where it is applied.\\
% A second remark is about the selection of the three parameters of a rolling window procedure: training length, test length, and stride. The choice depends completely on the properties of the data and, in spite of the efforts, we could not define a rule of thumb. Moreover, we are aware of the risk of false discoveries when trying multiple combinations of the parameters “until one works”. However, we derived this piece of advice: we found that it is essential to perform cross-validation, or backtesting, to have results that reflect the real performance of a forecasting algorithm. We tested a wide variety of window lengths from very short (10 days) to very long (500 days). We noticed that long windows present the non-stationarity problems mentioned in Section \ref{sec:sec22}, this is almost granted if the windows are longer than 90 days, but sometimes there are price level shifts that occur in much shorter times. However, splitting the data into many windows allows us to avoid more than one big shift per window or more than one (exponential) trend per window. Note that, if we were in a live trading context, it could be helpful to detect easily such a change or trend and promptly react by avoiding placing buy-sell actions. Hence, finally, we chose to compute the results on small windows, whose sizes vary between 15, 30 and 60 days. Having three different sizes lets us see at what point the forecasts of our models start to deteriorate.\\

\textbf{Evaluation Metrics}
%Hewamalage \emph{et al.} \cite{hewamalage} explores in detail the most common evaluation metrics for time series forecasting, each with its drawbacks. In our analysis, 
We used the classical Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) (\eqref{eq:rmse}). Further, it is easy to understand and popular in the previous studies \cite{kenny,lin}. 
\begin{align} \label{eq:rmse}
RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^n(y_t-\hat{y_t})^2}
\end{align}
Note that the RMSE is always computed over a test set of length 10 days (10 points) and is measured in US dollars.

\textbf{Hyperparameter Selection} Both ESN and XGBoost require tuning of their respective hyperparameters to achieve optimal results. We employed Bayesian Optimization, specifically Tree Parzen Estimators (TPE), using the widely adopted $hyperopt$ \footnote{https://hyperopt.github.io/hyperopt/} Python package, to search for the best hyperparameter combinations in their spaces. Table~\ref{table:ESN} and Table~\ref{table:XGB} describe the list of tuned parameters for ESN and XGB, respectively.

