\section{Conclusions and Future Work} 
\label{ch:conclusions}
%*********************************************************

% This research investigates the application of Reservoir Computing, specifically ESNs, for forecasting daily closing prices in cryptocurrency markets. The study highlights the promise of ESNs as a valuable method for addressing the complex and dynamic nature of these markets. By splitting the data into short windows, we verified that ESNs perform well across various market conditions, including strong upward or downward trends, sideways movements, and level shifts. This window-splitting approach mitigated challenges posed by high volatility and non-stationarity in price series. ESNs were effective with window sizes of 15, 30, and 60 days. Despite the high computational cost, extensive fine-tuning of ESNs resulted in accuracy surpassing that of state-of-the-art methods like XGBoost.

% The study also confirms that ESNs perform better in highly chaotic markets, as indicated by the Lyapunov exponent. This work challenges the Efficient Markets Hypothesis (EMH), which suggests the near-unpredictability of financial time series. Contrary to EMH, our findings suggest that effective forecasting can be achieved even in non-stationary and volatile markets.

% This research advances the field of cryptocurrency forecasting by demonstrating the effectiveness of ESNs through empirical evidence. The findings challenge conventional beliefs about market predictability and open new avenues for research and application in forecasting within highly dynamic and volatile financial conditions. Future work could consider extending this approach to other volatile cryptocurrencies beyond Bitcoin. Incorporating more external features with appropriate lags, possibly using methods like Convergent Cross Mapping \cite{Sugihara2012DetectingCI}, could enhance results. Implementing automatic feature selection and data preprocessing techniques could also improve accuracy. %Exploring Conformal Prediction for time series and Another natural next step would be to focus on multi-step ahead prediction \cite{Viehweg+others.2022} or trend direction. While this study was not aimed at creating a trading algorithm, it highlights the competitive potential of ESNs in forecasting under high volatility and non-stationarity.

This study explores the use of Reservoir Computing, particularly ESNs for forecasting daily cryptocurrency closing prices. ESNs proved effective across various market conditions including trends, sideways movements, and level shifts—by using a window-splitting approach that addressed volatility and non-stationarity. The results show that ESNs perform especially well in chaotic markets, as measured by the Lyapunov exponent, challenging the Efficient Markets Hypothesis (EMH) by demonstrating predictability in volatile financial series.

This work contributes empirical evidence for the viability of ESNs in cryptocurrency forecasting and opens new research directions. Future efforts may extend this approach to other cryptocurrencies, integrate lagged external features (e.g., via Convergent Cross Mapping), and explore techniques like automatic feature selection, multi-step forecasting, or trend prediction. While not designed as a trading algorithm, the study highlights the strong potential of ESNs under complex market dynamics.








% \textbf{Future Developments}. First off, we worked only with the Bitcoin dataset, but the work could be extended to other, even more volatile, cryptocurrencies. Secondly, the results could be improved by adding more external features, each with a proper lag. One way to compute such lags could be, remaining in the chaos theory framework, to use a Convergent Cross Mapping. It is a very powerful method that has not been applied often in the literature, perhaps due to its mathematical complexity, but has the potential to do so very well. Moreover, an automatic feature selection mechanism can be put into place to select the technical and external features. \\The results could also be improved by trying some of the data preprocessing methods we mentioned in the previous sections. Getting good forecasts is often a matter of tailored data manipulation and finding a good set of features. \\Data augmentation is also a way to possibly improve the predictions. The forecasting methods could be trained on larger datasets by including more cryptocurrencies and stocks, or even by using the same datasets but ensembling the predictions of methods running at different time frequencies. In general, ensembling models often improves the results and, in the literature, this is often done.\\ The quite recent Conformal Prediction for time series, which allows to calibrate the forecasts a posteriori, is also a possible research direction to explore. We briefly experimented with this and obtained a slight improvement in the errors. \\Since investors are usually trying to predict whether the price will go up or down in the next 5 to 7 days (or hours, in the case of day traders), an extension of the work could be about multi-step ahead prediction, rather than one-step ahead, which is not so applicable in a trading context. In this case, maybe, forecasting just the trend direction (binary or multi-class classification seems more suitable) could be more effective. Similarly, we could make a trading strategy based on the forecasts made by the methods. It is possible to create some rules to determine when to buy and when to sell a certain amount of Bitcoins. This strategy can be run by a bot and a trading session could virtually be simulated. This would make the problem much more interesting, as the loss functions could be adapted to maximize returns, instead of $L^2$ losses on the price forecasts.\\ Regarding the last two research directions, however, we want to stress that we did not follow them because our purpose was not to make an algorithm for trading. Rather, we aimed at challenging the state-of-the-art of time series forecasting in difficult circumstances such as in high volatility and high non-stationarity. The cryptocurrency price data was suitable for this matter and the ESN proved to be a competitive method to do forecasts.\\

